    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`. `pytest -q` passes (3 tests), and I checked all three cases against a real in-process MCP server.\n\n```python\ndef _text(result: CallToolResult) -> str:\n    \"\"\"The result's text blocks joined with newlines.\"\"\"\n    return \"\\n\".join(block.text for block in result.content if isinstance(block, TextContent))\n\n\nasync def run_tool(session: ClientSession, name: str, arguments: dict[str, Any]) -> Any:\n    \"\"\"Call tool `name` and return its structured output, or else its text.\"\"\"\n    result = await session.call_tool(name, arguments)\n    if result.is_error:\n        raise ToolCallFailed(name, _text(result))\n    if result.structured_content is not None:\n        return result.structured_content\n    return _text(result)\n```\n\n- **Context7 wasn't used:** it failed because its monthly quota was exceeded. Instead I checked the API against the installed `mcp==2.2.0` source. In this version the result fields are snake_case (`is_error`, `structured_content`), not the older `isError` / `structuredContent`.\n- **Structured check uses `is not None`:** the MCP spec version this SDK targets allows structured content to be any JSON value. A value like `0` or `[]` is still returned as structured output, not replaced by the text.\n- **Test results:**\n  - A tool with structured output returned `{'service': 'api', 'up': True}`.\n  - A text-only tool returned `'hello'`.\n  - A tool that raised an exception gave `ToolCallFailed('deploy', 'Error executing tool deploy')`. That message is exactly what the server sent back, because this server replaces the original exception text with a generic message.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** I couldn't delete my test script `_smoke.py` in the project root because the delete needs your approval. Please remove it yourself."
